  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:1",
      "id": "def-hilbert-samuel-multiplicity",
      "location": "Definition",
      "defect": "ill-formed",
      "evidence": "The definition permits an arbitrary finite module, then says: “let $d=\\deg P_{I,M}$” and defines $e_I(M):=d!\\cdot(\\text{leading coefficient})$. Take $R=k$, $M=0$, and $I=(0)$. This satisfies the stated hypotheses and has eventual Hilbert-Samuel polynomial $P_{I,M}=0$, whose degree is not a nonnegative integer; consequently $d!$ and the asserted leading coefficient are undefined. No nonzero hypothesis or zero-module convention is supplied.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "44840ad1b090e09958b9c4cfc42aa07f33b9b7bbaa53c90d2ad1e28038e3bc12"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:2",
      "id": "lem-rees-module-finiteness-and-stable-filtrations",
      "location": "Statement, clause 1; proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Clause 1 asserts the equivalence for every commutative ring, while step 1.1 assumes without justification that “$M_0,\\ldots,M_c$ are finite $R$-modules.” Let $R=k[x_1,x_2,\\ldots]$, let $J=(x_1,x_2,\\ldots)$, let $I=(0)$ and $M=R$, and filter by $M_0=R$, $M_1=J$, and $M_n=0$ for $n\\ge2$. This is an $I$-stable filtration from index $1$, but $\\mathcal R(I)=R$ and $\\mathcal R(M_\\bullet)=R\\oplus Jt$ is not finite over $R$, since $J$ is not finitely generated. Thus stability does not imply Rees-module finiteness under the written hypotheses.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "f24d09ab70da0943063420a82cbf2ca03b23c36c1be50e89c3f39403840b26f7"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:3",
      "id": "thm-existence-of-hilbert-samuel-polynomial",
      "location": "Proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 says: “Because $I$ is an ideal of definition, $R/I$ is Artinian.” The scoped definition of an ideal of definition requires only $M/IM$ to have finite length. For $R=k[[x,y]]$, $M=R/(x)$, and $I=(y)$, one has $M/IM\\cong k$ of finite length, but $R/I\\cong k[[x]]$ is not Artinian. The cited scoped Hilbert-Serre theorem requires an Artinian degree-zero ring, so this false assertion does not license the application in step 1.2.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "f2270d1604cbdd517e62ce58a790fff02c6153be2d0d92807e39c7ca12528cd6"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:4",
      "id": "thm-faithfully-flat-ring-map-characterisations",
      "location": "Proof step 1.3",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.3 chooses $\\mathfrak p\\subseteq\\mathfrak m$ and $\\mathfrak q_{\\mathfrak m}$ over $\\mathfrak m$, then asserts a localization $R_{\\mathfrak p}\\to S_{\\mathfrak q_{\\mathfrak m}}$. Such a map need not exist: for the identity $k[x]\\to k[x]$, take $\\mathfrak p=(0)$, $\\mathfrak m=\\mathfrak q_{\\mathfrak m}=(x)$. The source localization inverts $x$, while $x$ is not invertible in $k[x]_{(x)}$. Hence the claimed localized map, and therefore the asserted prime-lifting argument, is not defined.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "74f03be0767360389011b13420a360ffb9db85a4e938cb77b2a0bfd1ec9e3464"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:5",
      "id": "thm-hilbert-samuel-dimension-theorem",
      "location": "Proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 states: “Any two ideals of definition contain powers of one another.” Under the scoped definition, take $R=k[[x,y]]$, $M=R/(x)$, $I=(y)$, and $Q=(x,y)$. Both $M/IM$ and $M/QM$ are isomorphic to $k$, so both are ideals of definition for $M$. But $Q^r$ contains $x^r$, which is not in $I^s$ for any $r,s\\ge1$. Thus these ideals do not contain powers of one another as ideals of $R$, and the stated comparison does not establish the degree-replacement inference.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "b0b7bf1aa85ff9d09146e07ecf702dffd729b89b87d8335a4e287fc6e265ac92"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:6",
