      "evidence": "Step 1.3 establishes only $K_J\\subseteq I(J\\otimes_RM)$. Step 1.4 then says that because elements of $K_J$ map to zero in $M$, this “may be sharpened to $K_J\\subseteq IK_J$.” Kernel membership and containment in $I(J\\otimes_RM)$ do not imply containment in $IK_J$: for the quotient map $\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb Z/2$, with $I=(2)$ and kernel $K=2\\mathbb Z$, one has $K=I\\mathbb Z$ but $IK=4\\mathbb Z$, so $K\\nsubseteq IK$. The cited flatness criterion only reduces flatness to vanishing of these kernels; it does not provide the asserted sharpening. The needed local-criterion argument is absent.",
